        "id": "ch11_3_3-1",
        "text": "Chapter 11 I Windows Privilege Escalation\n297\nExploiting Services in Windows\nServices in the Windows system are like daemons in the Linux OS. They run\ntasks in the background, and most of the time, they execute with SYSTEM priv-\nileges (giving us another way to achieve a system shell).\nInteracting with Windows Services\nWe will need to interact with the Windows services through the command line\n(since we have a limited shell). To get the job done, we will encounter the fol-\nlowing popular commands:\n#To List the configuration of a service\nC:\\>sc.exe qc [service name]\n#To edit the settings of a service\nC:\\>sc.exe config [service name] [setting]=[new value]\n#To Stop a service\nC:\\>sc.exe stop [service name]\n#To Start a service\nC:\\>sc.exe start [service name]\n#To get the status of a service\nC:\\>sc.exe query [service name]\nMisconfigured Service Permissions\nIn this scenario, we\u2019re looking for services with the wrong permissions. In other\nwords, we want a service where we (limited users) have permission to change\nits configuration. Here are the two common settings that should allow us to\noverride a service (allowing us to get a remote shell):\nI\nI SERVICE_CHANGE_CONFIG\nI\nI SERVICE_ALL_ACCESS\nGenerally, you can use an automated tool to find a misconfigured service.\nLater in this chapter, you will learn about some of the common tools, but for\nthe time being, focus on the principle of its functionality.\nFor example, the automated tool found a misconfigured service called miss_\nsvc . In your command prompt, use the sc.exe utility to query its configuration:\nC:\\Users\\LowPrivUser> sc qc miss_svc\n[SC] QueryServiceConfig SUCCESS\nSERVICE_NAME: miss_svc\nTYPE                               : 10  WIN32_OWN_PROCESS",
